<p>Pop PDF is a PHP library for working with PDF documents in a number of different ways. You can
<strong>build</strong> PDFs from the ground up - or hand it HTML to have it do the work for you. You can <strong>import</strong>
existing PDFs and build on them. Or, you <strong>extract</strong> only the parts you need, such as text or images
for further processing.</p>
<h2 id="what-it-does">What It Does<a class="anchor" href="#what-it-does" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>

<p>A <code>Document</code> holds pages, and a page holds the things you put on it — text, fonts, images and vector
paths and shapes. Build that structure by hand, one call per element, when a layout needs exact
placement. Or skip the object calls entirely and pass a string of HTML to <code>Pdf::importFromHtml()</code>,
which parses it, applies any CSS it carries, and returns the same kind of <code>Document</code> laid out for you.
Either route ends at the same object: write it to a file, stream it to a browser, or read it back
with <code>(string) $document</code>.</p>
<p>The same library reads PDFs it did not create. <code>Pdf::importFromFile()</code> opens an existing document
and turns its pages, fonts and images into the same objects a document you built holds, so you can
add a page, stamp existing ones, or merge several files into one and save the result. Reach the other
direction and <code>Pdf::extractTextFromFile()</code> pulls the text layer back out as a string, while
<code>Pdf::extractAsImages()</code> rasterizes each page to a JPEG, PNG, WebP or TIFF file.</p>
<p>Building, importing and extracting are all native PHP — the object model that represents a document,
the code that writes PDF's own binary format, and the parser that reads it back in are not a wrapper
around a system binary or a third-party PDF engine. The one exception is rasterizing pages to image
files: that path hands the actual pixel rendering to the <code>imagick</code> extension. Every other capability
on this site runs with nothing installed beyond Pop PDF itself and the PHP extensions most installs
already carry.</p>
<h2 id="build-import-extract">Build, Import, Extract<a class="anchor" href="#build-import-extract" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>

<p>Multiple features, one component, instead of having to install multiple components to manage all things PDF:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Build</strong>
<ul>
<li>Assemble a document from pages, text, fonts, images and drawn paths, style it with
color, compression and metadata, and save it to a file, an HTTP response or a string. Start at
<a href="/build/documents">Creating Documents</a>.</li>
<li>Pass valid HTML and CSS to the HTML parser and have it create the object model of the document
and everything contained within. Start at <a href="/build/html">HTML to PDF</a>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Import</strong> — open an existing PDF as editable objects, add pages to it, stamp text or images onto
pages it already has, or merge several files into one. Start at <a href="/import/importing">Importing</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Extract</strong> — pull the text layer out of a PDF, tell scanned pages apart from ones with real text,
and rasterize pages to standalone image files. Start at <a href="/extract/text">Extracting Text</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each of these features stands on its own — a script that only extracts text never touches the build side
— and all three share the same <code>Document</code> and <code>Page</code> objects, so a document you built and one you
imported look identical to any code that works on them afterward.</p>
<h2 id="where-to-start">Where to Start<a class="anchor" href="#where-to-start" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>

<p><a href="/installation">Installation</a> covers the Composer require line and a one-file check that everything
is wired up. <a href="/quickstart">Quickstart</a> walks through five short scripts — text placed by hand, a
page built from HTML, an image, importing and stamping a file, and extracting text back out — each
one complete on its own. If you want control over where every element lands, start with <a href="/build/documents">Creating
Documents</a> and work through Build page by page; if you want to hand over a string
of HTML and get a PDF back, start at <a href="/build/html">HTML to PDF</a> and skip the rest of Build entirely.</p>
<p>From there, the sidebar splits into Build, Import and Extract, matching the three things above.
Build is organized by what you are adding to a page — text, fonts, images, paths — with HTML to PDF
as the parser-driven alternative to all of it, and Import and Extract are short enough to read end
to end.</p>
